/**
 * Normalizes Contact.Phone values for the dialer.
 *
 * The dialer accepts only digits and a leading plus sign, so every other
 * character is stripped from each non-blank Phone before the records are
 * saved in a single, partial-success DML call.
 */
public with sharing class ContactPhoneCleanup {

    /**
     * Strips every character that is not a digit or '+' from the Phone of the
     * supplied Contacts and saves the changed records in one bulk DML call.
     *
     * @param contactIds Ids of the Contacts to normalize (up to 200 per call).
     * @return Map of Contact Id -> first error message for every record that
     *         could not be saved; empty when every record saved successfully.
     */
    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> failures = new Map<Id, String>();

        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        // De-duplicate and drop nulls so the SOQL bind stays clean.
        Set<Id> idsToProcess = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id contactId : contactIds) {
            if (contactId != null) {
                idsToProcess.add(contactId);
            }
        }
        if (idsToProcess.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        List<Contact> contacts = [
            SELECT Id, Phone
            FROM Contact
            WHERE Id IN :idsToProcess
            WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED
        ];

        // Only contacts that actually have a phone are changed/updated.
        List<Contact> contactsToUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact c : contacts) {
            if (String.isNotBlank(c.Phone)) {
                c.Phone = c.Phone.replaceAll('[^0-9+]', '');
                contactsToUpdate.add(c);
            }
        }

        if (contactsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        // Single, partial-success DML: one bad record does not block the rest.
        for (Database.SaveResult sr : Database.update(contactsToUpdate, false)) {
            if (!sr.isSuccess()) {
                failures.put(sr.getId(), sr.getErrors()[0].getMessage());
            }
        }

        return failures;
    }
}
```

## What I kept and what I changed

Your core approach is sound, and the method does exactly what you asked: one query, strip non-digit/non-`+` characters, one `Database.update(..., false)`, then map each failed record to its first error message (empty map on full success). I kept that structure verbatim. The deviations, and why:

1. **`WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED` on the query** — the method runs in the caller's context; without this, a user who can't read `Contact.Phone` would still get records processed in system mode. This enforces object/field read access so we don't operate on data the running user can't see. (If you're on API 59+, `WITH USER_MODE` is the newer equivalent; `WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED` is used here for broad compatibility. Either way, note the DML itself is still system-mode because the required `Database.update(contacts, false)` overload has no access-level argument — if you want full user-mode DML, say so and I'll switch to `Database.update(records, AccessLevel.USER_MODE)`.)

2. **`public with sharing`** — a class that reads and writes Contact should respect the org's sharing rules rather than silently running in system mode.

3. **`String.isNotBlank(c.Phone)` instead of `!= null`** — "contacts without a phone" realistically includes whitespace-only values; those would otherwise normalize to `''` and be written back. Pure blanks are now left out. Non-blank junk like `"( )"` still normalizes to `''` and is saved, matching your literal instruction to strip every non-digit/non-`+` character.

4. **De-dupe + null-guard the input** — a `Set<Id>` removes duplicate Ids and drops nulls before the SOQL bind, avoiding a wasted scan and any null-binding edge case. It does not change the returned map, which is keyed by Id.

5. **Early returns** for null/empty input and for "nothing to update," so we never issue an empty DML call (which would throw `DmlException`).

One residual behavior worth flagging: the regex keeps a `+` **anywhere** in the string, not just as the first character, because that's exactly what you specified. If the dialer truly requires the plus only as a leading character, we should instead normalize to `'+' + digits` or reject mid-string `+`; tell me and I'll tighten it.

